            </div>
            <h3>CO₂ Paradox</h3>
            <p>High CO₂ emitters span all income groups unexpectedly. The US and Australia (high-income) emit 14–15 tons/capita, while UAE and Saudi Arabia emit 18–22 tons, yet lower-middle income Vietnam and India emit only 1.9–3.5 tons — income does not linearly determine carbon footprint.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="insight-card">
            <div class="insight-icon" style="background:rgba(59,130,246,0.12);">
              <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#3b82f6" stroke-width="2"><path d="M17 21v-2a4 4 0 0 0-4-4H5a4 4 0 0 0-4 4v2"/><circle cx="9" cy="7" r="4"/><path d="M23 21v-2a4 4 0 0 0-3-3.87"/><path d="M16 3.13a4 4 0 0 1 0 7.75"/></svg>
            </div>
            <h3>Upper-Middle Divergence</h3>
            <p>Upper-middle income nations show the widest variance, particularly in Freedom (China: 9 vs. Brazil: 73) and CO₂ emissions (Colombia: 1.8 vs. Russia: 11.4). This group contains both democratic and authoritarian states, unlike high-income nations which cluster toward freedom.</p>
          </article>
